# Documentation Source Structure

This file is named `README` so fsdocs ignores it while it still stays next
to the documentation source it describes.

This folder is the fsdocs source tree for the ARC Abstract Model documentation.
Most pages are ordinary Markdown; literate F# scripts can also be compiled
and evaluated during `BuildDocs`.

## Main Areas

- `index.md` is the documentation landing page.
- `project/` contains user-facing project guides: overview, implementation
  notes, querying background, references, examples, schemas, and prior art.
- `spec/` contains the normative specification. The three ARC Abstract Model
  profiles live under `spec/provenance_core/`, `spec/semantic_core/`, and
  `spec/administrative_core/`. Entity specifications shared unchanged between
  Abstract Model profiles live under `spec/shared/`; decoration profiles, including Datamap,
  live under `spec/decorations/`.
- `_head.html` is injected into every generated page and is the place for small
  site-wide presentation adapters that cannot be expressed in page frontmatter.
- `output/` is generated by fsdocs and ignored by git.

## Page Metadata

fsdocs uses YAML frontmatter to build the sidebar:

```yaml
---
title: Page Title
category: Project
categoryindex: 2
index: 1
---
```

Use `category` for the sidebar group, `categoryindex` to order groups, and
`index` to order pages inside a group. Keep normative field definitions in
`spec/`; use `project/` for didactic guides and
library usage.

## `_head.html` Customization

`_head.html` currently adds three site-wide behaviors:

- Mermaid support: code fences marked as Mermaid are converted to Mermaid
  diagram blocks before Mermaid renders them.
- YAML highlighting: raw generated YAML blocks marked with `language-yaml` or
  `lang="yaml"` are highlighted in the browser with Highlight.js.
- Sidebar regrouping: fsdocs emits a flat list of category headers and links.
  A small DOM transform makes `Project` collapsible, groups the Abstract Model
  profile sections under a synthetic `Abstract Model Profiles` heading, and groups the decoration
  sections under a synthetic `Decorations` heading. It also places each
  group's overview/info page first and makes each subsection collapsible. The
  active subsection opens automatically on its own pages. If generated output
  pages leak into an `Other` category during local watch, duplicate links are
  removed only when the same page already appears elsewhere in the menu. The
  same transform is applied to the desktop sidebar and the mobile menu.

Prefer frontmatter ordering when possible. Use `_head.html` only for behavior
that fsdocs cannot express directly, and keep comments beside any custom DOM
logic so future template changes are easier to audit.

## Generated Mermaid In Literate Scripts

For static diagrams in Markdown or literate comments, use a normal Mermaid code
fence:

````markdown
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A --> B
```
````

For diagrams generated by F# code in a `.fsx` page, emit raw Mermaid HTML:

```fsharp
(*** hide ***)
let mermaidBlock (text: string) =
    text.Trim()
    |> System.Net.WebUtility.HtmlEncode
    |> sprintf "<pre class=\"mermaid\">%s</pre>"

let graph =
    """
flowchart LR
  Extraction --> Leaf
  Extraction --> ExtractData
"""

mermaidBlock graph
(*** include-it-raw ***)
```

The `_head.html` Mermaid adapter renders every `<pre class="mermaid">...</pre>`
block, whether it came from a Markdown fence or from generated raw HTML.

## Build Commands

```powershell
.\build.cmd BuildDocs
.\build.cmd WatchDocs
dotnet fsdocs watch --output docs/output --eval --clean
```

`BuildDocs` evaluates literate F# pages, so NuGet restore and local package
availability affect the build.

